WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:05.660
You want your business emails to appear professional
and use the right language.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:12.320
It’s especially important when applying
for jobs online, as you want to make a good

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.340
first impression.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:20.800
Here are some simple tips to help you with
both formal and informal messages.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:27.650
Before you begin writing your email, consider
whether you know the reader or not.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.550
When you don’t know the reader well or you
want to be polite, it’s best to be formal

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:41.030
- for example, business emails, job applications,
and emails to big companies.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:48.260
If you know the reader well and it’s not
a business context, you can use informal language.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.200
You would probably be informal if you were
sending a birthday message to a friend,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.800
for example.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.000
Of course, the formality of the situation
may depend on the country or culture

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.200
you’re in.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.420
It’s a good idea to get familiar with that
culture before deciding how formal or informal

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.600
you want to be.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:18.490
A formal email in the UK will usually begin
with a formal starting phrase, such as ‘Dear

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:24.600
Mr Piper’, or ‘Dear Sir or Madam’ if
you don’t know the name of the reader.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:31.409
You should also use an appropriate formal
ending phrase, such as ‘Yours sincerely’

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:36.420
when you know the reader, or ‘Yours faithfully’
when you don’t.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.649
You can use more casual phrases in an informal
email.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.279
If you’re sending a message to a friend
or family member, you might begin with a simple

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:52.400
‘Hi’ or ‘Hello’, and finish with ‘Thanks’,
‘See you soon’, or ‘Cheers’.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:02.029
It is conventional to use a comma and start
a new line after any opening and closing phrases.

00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:06.729
This is true whether you are being formal
or informal.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:13.980
You should also be careful using slang phrases
or emojis, as these are very informal.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:21.440
For example, you should never use a smiley
face in a job application, even if you want

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.200
to show that you are a very friendly person.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:31.820
You should also avoid typing in all capitals,
as it looks like you are shouting.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.840
[shouting] YOU WOULDN’T TALK LIKE THIS IN
AN OFFICE, so why do it in an email?

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.120
Subscribe to our channel for more videos on
how to improve your English language skills.

